
## Task 1: DNS – How Names Become IPs
🌐 What happens when you type google.com in a browser?

The browser checks its cache and OS cache for the IP.

If not found, it queries a DNS resolver (ISP or public DNS).

The resolver contacts root → TLD → authoritative name servers.

The IP address is returned, and the browser connects to that IP.

DNS Record Types

A — Maps a domain to an IPv4 address

AAAA — Maps a domain to an IPv6 address

CNAME — Alias pointing one domain to another domain

MX — Specifies mail servers for email delivery

NS — Identifies authoritative name servers for the domain

Run:
dig google.com

Look for:

A Record: IPv4 address (e.g., 142.250.x.x)

TTL: Time DNS result is cached (e.g., 300 seconds)

Example:

google.com.    300    IN    A    142.250.192.14
✅ Task 2: IP Addressing
What is an IPv4 address?

An IPv4 address is a 32-bit numeric identifier used to identify devices on a network.

Example:

192.168.1.10

Structure:

Four octets (0–255)

Each octet = 8 bits

Public vs Private IP
Type	Description	Example
Public	Routable on internet	8.8.8.8
Private	Used inside networks	192.168.1.5
Private IP Ranges

10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255

172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255

192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255

Run:
ip addr show

👉 Identify private IP (usually like 192.168.x.x or 10.x.x.x).

✅ ## Task 3: CIDR & Subnetting
What does /24 mean in 192.168.1.0/24?

It means 24 bits are used for the network, leaving 8 bits for hosts.

Usable Hosts
CIDR	Total IPs	Usable Hosts
/24	256	254
/16	65,536	65,534
/28	16	14
Why do we subnet?

Improves network organization

Enhances security segmentation

Reduces broadcast traffic

Efficient IP allocation

Fill in Table
CIDR	Subnet Mask	Total IPs	Usable Hosts
/24	255.255.255.0	256	254
/16	255.255.0.0	65,536	65,534
/28	255.255.255.240	16	14

✅ Task 4: Ports – The Doors to Services
What is a port?

A port is a logical communication endpoint that allows multiple services to run on one machine.

Common Ports
Port	Service
22	SSH
80	HTTP
443	HTTPS
53	DNS
3306	MySQL
6379	Redis
27017	MongoDB
Run:
ss -tulpn

Example:

LISTEN 0 128 0.0.0.0:22    sshd
LISTEN 0 128 127.0.0.1:6379 redis

✅ Task 5: Putting It Together
curl http://myapp.com:8080
 — what happens?

DNS resolves myapp.com → IP

TCP connects to port 8080

HTTP request sent to web service

Server responds with content

Concepts involved:
DNS → IP → TCP → Port → HTTP

